---
description: Code style and TypeScript conventions
alwaysApply: true
---

## Functions

- Use arrow functions (`const fn = () => {}`) over named function declarations in
  application code. Preserve upstream function declarations in the dashboard's
  registry-managed shadcn files (`components/ui/**`) so registry updates remain
  reviewable.

## Comments

**Always enforced:** do not add inline comments, JSDoc, or section banners that restate what the code already says. If a comment only repeats the function name, variable name, or the next few lines of logic, delete it.

- Comment only what the code can't reveal on its own — non-obvious "why", subtle invariants, workarounds, gotchas, a contract another module or future integration relies on.
- Do **not** comment every function, block, or export. Prefer self-explanatory names over comments.
- Do not reference product screen/task numbers (`#10`, `#11`, …) in code comments — those are product identifiers, not technical ones. Describe the behavior in words.
- Keep JSDoc (`/** */`) blocks to roughly **3 lines** by default. A longer block is
  allowed only for a non-obvious cross-module contract, invariant, or workaround
  that would be unsafe to lose; move reusable policy into `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`.
- Section dividers (`// ─── … ───`) are allowed in large route/service files when they group unrelated blocks; do not add them to small modules or new files.

## Code Style (Biome)

- 2-space indentation, single quotes, always semicolons.
- 100 character line width.
- Exclude generated files (such as `routeTree.gen.ts`) from linting/formatting in `biome.json`.

## Path Aliases

- Use `~/` when an application import crosses feature/domain boundaries (e.g.
  `import { Button } from '~/components/ui/button'`). Relative imports are allowed
  within one cohesive module tree, in registry-managed files, and from root build or
  Worker entrypoints where the relative graph is clearer.
